---
name: ptt-pr-draft-reply
description: "Draft the answer to a review of this branch's local request for the user to check: the planned change per comment and the full reply text. Writes and releases nothing; /ptt-pr-reply releases it. Argument: none or the request number N."
---

# Draft a reply to a review

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes (it writes none); other work needs AtE (rules file).

The branch is the one the user names; otherwise the branch of the worktree
this holder's task works in (`worktrees/<topic>`; if several, list them and
ask). Run every git command from the workspace root with `git -C
<worktree>`; never ask the user to change directory. Request numbers may be
short (`4`, `pr-4`, `000004`). Without N, the request is this branch's one
open request waiting for its author; if there is none or several, list them
(`<records>/bin/pr list all --branch BRANCH`) and ask. With N, the request
must be in `pr/open`: if `<records>/bin/pr list all` shows it merged or
closed, report its state and decision and stop. A decided request takes no
draft and no reply, and a pending draft of it lapses; a follow-up is a new
request (`/ptt-pr-submit`).

1. Read `<records>/bin/pr show N` and the code on the branch.
2. Show, per comment ID, the planned change (a diff where short) or why
   you disagree; then the full reply text (the template in
   `ptt-pr-reply`) in a fenced block (```` ```markdown ````; with more
   backticks than any fence inside it), so that it displays as it will be
   written. Values that exist only after carrying out the plan (commit
   SHAs, run ids, test counts) are `{fill: what}`, e.g. `{fill: commit
   SHA}`, with the expected result outside the block.
3. Write nothing: no file, no commit, no recorded message. End with: "To
   release it, run `/ptt-pr-reply NNNNNN`; say first what to change, if
   anything."
